===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# ''A [[dwelling]].''
## One’s own [[dwell]]ing place; the [[house]] or structure in which one [[live]]s; especially the house in which one lives with one's [[family]]; the habitual [[abode]] of one’s family.
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|John|20|10|clj|And the diſciples went awaye agayne vnto their awne '''home'''.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1808|author=w:John Dryden|editor=w:Walter Scott|title=The Works of John Dryden|passage=Thither for ease and soft repose we come: / '''Home''' is the sacred refuge of our life; / Secured from all approaches, but a wife.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1822|author=w:John Howard Payne|title=w:Home! Sweet Home!|passage='''Home'''! '''home'''! sweet, sweet '''home'''! / There’s no place like '''home''', there’s no place like '''home'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1981|author=w:William Irwin Thompson|title=The Time Falling Bodies Take to Light: Mythology, Sexuality and the Origins of Culture|publisher=Rider/Hutchinson & Co.|location=London|page=132|passage=If we now say that "woman's place is in the '''home'''," it is not because men put her there, but because the '''home''' became the capitol of women's mysteries.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-29|volume=407|issue=8842|page=28|magazine=w:The Economist|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/asia/21580209-natural-disaster-aggravated-poor-response-high-and-wet High and wet]|passage=Rock-filled torrents smashed vehicles and '''homes''', burying victims under rubble and sludge.}}
## The place (residence, settlement, country, etc.), where a person was [[born]] or [[raise]]d; a [[childhood]] or [[parental]] home; the home of one’s parents or [[guardian]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2004|author=Jean Harrison|title='''Home'''|passage=The rights listed in the UNCRC cover all areas of children's lives such as their right to have a '''home''' and their right to be educated.}}
##: {{ux|en|Does she still live at '''home'''? - No, she moved out and got an apartment when she was 18, but she still lives in the city.}}
## The [[abiding]] place of the [[affection]]s, especially of the [[domestic]] affections.
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1821|author=w:George Gordon Byron|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/21700/21700-h/21700-h.htm|title=Don Juan|section=canto III|passage=He enter’d in the house—his '''home''' no more, / For without hearts there is no '''home''';{{...}}}}
##* {{quote-song|en|year=1977|album=Keith Barrow|author=w:Keith Barrow|title=Precious|text=You only live life one time / Thank God I'll never live alone / My heart will always have a '''home''' / Living with you}}
## A [[house]] that has been made [[homelike]], to [[suit]] the [[comfort]] of those who live there.
##: {{ux|en|It's what you bring into a house that makes it a '''home'''}}
## A place of [[refuge]], [[rest]] or [[care]]; an [[asylum]].
##: {{ux|en|a '''home''' for outcasts}}
##: {{ux|en|a '''home''' for the blind}}
##: {{ux|en|a veterans' '''home'''}}
##: ''Instead of a [[pet store]], get your new dog from the local dogs’ '''home'''''.
##: {{ux|en|Our grandmama's moved into a '''home'''.}}
## {{lb|en|by extension}} The grave; the final rest; also, the native and eternal dwelling place of the soul.
##* '''1769''', King James Bible, Oxford Standard text, [[s:Bible (King James)/Ecclesiastes|Ecclesiastes]] 12:5:
##*: {{...}}because man goeth to his long '''home''', and the mourners go about the streets: {{...}}
## {{lb|en|by extension}} Anything that serves the functions of a home, as comfort, safety, sense of belonging, etc.
##* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Workers World|author=Leslie Feinberg|url=https://ia804705.us.archive.org/23/items/lavender-and-red/Lavender_and_Red.pdf#page=295|title=1976 WWP pamphlet found answers in Marxism|date=10 Jan 07|text=The rights of modern transsexual women and men to live in the sex that is "'''home'''".}}
# One’s [[native]] land; the place or [[country]] in which one dwells; the place where one’s ancestors dwell or dwelt.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1863|author=w:Nathaniel Hawthorne|title=Our Old '''Home''': A Series of English Sketches|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8090/pg8090.txt|passage=Visiting these famous localities, and a great many others, I hope that I do not compromise my American patriotism by acknowledging that I was often conscious of a fervent hereditary attachment to the native soil of our forefathers, and felt it to be our own Old '''Home'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1889|year_published=1899|author={{w|Rudyard Kipling}}|title={{w|Under the Deodars}}|chapter=The Hill of Illusion|location=Boston|publisher=The Greenock Press|page=93|text=Have you ''any'' people at '''Home''', Guy, to be pleased with your performances?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|author=James George Frazer|title=Folk-Lore In The Old Testament|volume=1|page=178|text=It seems possible that {{...}} the ancestors of the gipsies brought the legend with them to Transylvania from their old '''home''' in India.}}
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|IV|passage=So this was my future '''home''', I thought! Certainly it made a brave picture. I had seen similar ones fired-in on many a Heidelberg stein. Backed by towering hills,{{...}}a sky of palest Gobelin flecked with fat, fleecy little clouds, it in truth looked a dear little city; the city of one's dreams.}}
#* '''1980''', {{w|Peter Allen}}, song, ''{{w|I Still Call Australia Home}}'':
#*: I've been to cities that never close down / From New York to Rio and old London town / But no matter how far or how wide I roam / I still call Australia '''home'''.
# The [[locality]] where a thing is usually found, or was first found, or where it is naturally abundant; [[habitat]]; [[seat]].
#: {{ux|en|the '''home''' of the pine}}
#* '''1706''', {{w|Matthew Prior}}, ''An Ode, Humbly Inscribed to the Queen, on the ẛucceẛs of Her Majeẛty's Arms, 1706'', as republished in '''1795''', Robert Anderson (editor), ''The Works of the British Poets'':
#*: {{...}}Flandria, by plenty made the '''home''' of war, / Shall weep her crime, and bow to Charles r'estor'd,{{nb...}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1849|author=w:Alfred Tennyson|title=s:In Memoriam A. H. H.|passage=Her eyes are '''homes''' of silent prayer, / Nor other thought her mind admits / But, he was dead, and there he sits, / And he that brought him back is there.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-09-07|volume=408|issue=8852|magazine=w:The Economist|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21584965-two-nasty-childhood-illnesses-africa-are-coming-under-scientific Nodding acquaintance]|passage=Africa is '''home''' to so many premier-league diseases (such as AIDS, childhood diarrhoea, malaria and tuberculosis) that those in lower divisions are easily ignored.}}
