====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|of the weather}} To be [[violent]], with [[wind]]s of 119 km/h (74 miles per hour) or greater, usually accompanied by [[rain]], [[lightning]], and [[thunder]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1872|month=September|work=Fraser's Magazine |title=The Misadventures of Mr. Catlyne, Q.C.|author=Matthew Stradling|volume=|page=375|passage=Through the night it rained, '''hurricaned'''; sashes rattled; the chimney smoked; a lobby-door was ajar, and kept banging to and fro.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1897| title=Marginal Notes: In Indian Ink |author=Philip Neville | page=261| passage=So I got inside my 'possum-skin—it '''hurricaned''' an' snowed— And I started for the Deepot, and lit out for his abode.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1907| title=The Rejuvenation of Aunt Mary |author=Anne Warner | page=188| passage=I guess it did storm. I guess it '''hurricaned'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980| title=Hearings, Reports and Prints of the House Committee on Interior and Insular Affairs|author=United States. Congress. House. Committee on Interior and Insular Affairs| page=105| passage=He is substituting for Rudy Black, who is our Resource Manager, who was '''hurricaned''' out of an appearance today because he could not get back from vacation.}}
# To [[move]] [[noisily]], [[quickly]], and [[dramatically]], becoming the center of [[attention]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1911| title=Account Rendered |author=Edward Frederic Benson | page=24| passage=While thus employed, Mrs. Winthrop '''hurricaned''' into the room, and having received explanations, '''hurricaned''' forth again to tell her husband how dreadfully thoughtless his arrangement was, since Frank would have to take Miss Allenby into dinner. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014| title=Children of the Streets|author=Harlan Ellison | page=| passage=They must have told him I was ready for visitors; he '''hurricaned''' into the room, slung a chair away from the wall and banged it down next to the bed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022| title=Judokill|author=Bill Watson | page=| passage=The Super nodded wisely, 'I agree, constable. See to it, Forbes!' and with that  '''hurricaned''' out.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2023| title=Now Then: A Biography of Yorkshire|author=Rick Broadbent | page=| passage=Angela Stripe was a force of nature. Bold and brave and brassy in a warm way, she never breezed in, she '''hurricaned'''.}}
# To attempt to [[accomplish]] a great deal with a [[frenzied]] [[effort]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011| title=Stumbling Toward Enlightenment |author=Geri Larkin | page=81| passage=There were still spots on the mirror I had cleaned and tiny bits of newspaper were stuck in its edges where I had '''hurricaned''' through in my initial cleaning frenzy. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012| title=Age of Anansi|author=James Lovegrove | page=| passage="A clean home is a good home," she'd say as she '''hurricaned''' from room to room with vacuum cleaner and feather duster, hands gauntleted in Marigolds.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020| title=The Single Mums' Secrets|author=Janet Hoggarth | page=| passage=Lara was running in and out of shops, her brain buzzing, ticking off items as she '''hurricaned''' through open doors to the sound of jingle bells and fifties crooners. }}
# To [[swirl]] quickly and violently.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014| title=Heroism in the New Black Poetry: Introductions and Interviews|author=D.H. Melhem | page=| passage=A whole swirl of turnarounds '''hurricaned''' from him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020| title=Terminal Reality|author=Erik Seedhouse | page=| passage=Kona responded as air '''hurricaned''' out of the bay and water rushed in.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021| title=Mystery's|author=Will Sly | page=20| passage=Like a cadaver dog, she latched onto his scent again and when it '''hurricaned''' around in her cerebral fluids with those dirty, honest, direct thoughts on capturing her lover, she couldn't stop herself from taking aggressive action.}}
# To [[tear apart]] in a violent, [[destructive]], and/or [[chaotic]] manner.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1770| title=The Dramatic Censor|author=Francis Gentleman | page=429| passage=Urganda, '''hurricaned''' with violent perturbation of mind commences the fourth act; and, after a soliloquy, or rather incantation, raises a dæmon of revenge;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009| title=Love and War in London: A Woman’s Diary 1939-1942 |author=Robert W. Malcolmson, Olivia Cockett | page=15| passage=My personal life has been '''hurricaned''' in the last month, not by the war particularly, but by having to decide not to “live in sin” after trying it for a few days.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009| title=Trauma Farm: A Rebel History of Rural Life|author=Brian Brett | page=| passage=Sharon gratefully assumed I'd '''hurricaned''' my last kitchen with my flour-tossing talents. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015| title=The Mind of Africa |author=W.E Abraham| page=157| passage=And within the Soviet Union itself, it is said that some of its underdeveloped territories were '''hurricaned''' by revolutionary dynamics from a feudal to a socialist structure. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017| title=Army Fundamentals|author=Bethan Greener | page=| passage=The other girls whispered to us 'He's '''hurricaning''' the room.'}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2023| title=Chicana Portraits|author=Norma Elia Cantú | page=189| passage=Though Tafolla has always addressed the themes of suffering, persevering, and surviving within her expansive body of work, many of her writings exploring what she refers to as that "'''hurricaned'''" time period of cancer, the mastectomy, her role as a “cancer veteran," and the "Trinity of Deaths" remain in stacks of papers and handwritten journals, polished but percolating patiently along with some of her unfinished works—her scurrying utterances found clutched to the edges of crumple receipts, scratched onto torn envelopes, and etched into the surfaces of folders, book covers, and small boxes piling up on top of her desk.}}
# To cause an [[uproar]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1678| title=The pilgrim's progress|author=John Bunyan| page=| passage=What do they therefore but quickly get themselves into a body, and fall forthwith to  '''hurricaning''' in Mansoul, as if now nothing but whirlwind and tempest should be there.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011| title=The Blue Light Project|author=Timothy Taylor | page=78| passage=It blew open. It '''hurricaned''' onto shore.}}
# To [[storm]] excessively; to [[fume]] and [[rage]] violently.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898| title=A Few Smiles |author=William Carter Platts | page=72| passage=He rang for the cook, and stormed and '''hurricaned''' and cycloned at her until the rain-clouds burst, and she wept.}}
# To [[chase]] [[violently]] or [[rush]] [[after]] and [[force]] along.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918| title=Adventure - Volume 17, Part 2 |author=| page=180| passage=As he '''hurricaned''' toward her, she turned and ran to the drop-off place.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010| title=Black Lions: A First on Ashanti |author=Melvin F. Compton | page=366| passage=Then they would all be in a confused clutter and cluster there, and there would be a mad stampede when The Black Watch '''hurricaned''' on their heels from behind, and several bags, boxes and barrels, if not virtually all, would fall in their wild rush for The Pra, their wounded and sick abandoned in a blood filled litter and sprawl over the raging sands.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014| title=Doctor At Large |author=Richard Gordon | page=99| passage=Was there a sudden general strike, perhaps, sweeping up the doctors and nurses as it '''hurricaned''' upon them?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019| title=The Man Who Stories |author=Robert Radnor | page=205| passage=I'll get swirled up in it. Tornadoed. '''Hurricaned'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019| title=The Kill Riff|author=David J. Schow | page=| passage=The One Stop clerk, a college student named Abel Langtry, gawked at the car chase as it '''hurricaned''' past in the rain.}}

