#: {{ux|en|A tall building was visible on the whole sweep of the '''horizon'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The sky and sea seem to merge at the '''horizon'''.}} 
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} The [[range]] or [[limit]] of one's [[knowledge]], [[experience]] or [[interest]]; a [[boundary]] or [[threshold]].
#: {{ux|en|Some students take a [[gap year]] after finishing [[high school]] to broaden their '''horizons'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|With clinical researchers hard [[at work]], a new treatment is [[on the horizon|on the '''horizon''']].}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1949|author={{w|Friedrich Hayek|F. A. Hayek}}|title=The Intellectuals and Socialism|journal={{w|University of Chicago Law Review}}|publisher={{w|University of Chicago}}|location={{w|Chicago}}|volume=16|number=3|doi=10.2307/1597903|page=428|passage=It is because theirs has become the only explicit general philosophy of social policy held by a large group, the only system or theory which raises new problems and opens new '''horizons''', that they have succeeded in inspiring the imagination of the intellectual.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1972|author1=w:Mortimer J. Adler|author2=w:Charles Van Doren|title=w:How to Read a Book|text=They learn that reading, besides being something one does at school, is also something one can do on one's own, for fun, to satisfy curiosity, or even to "expand one's '''horizons'''.”|isbn=0-671-21209-5|lccn=72-81451|page=25|pageurl=https://books.google.co.in/books?id=Z5PpkQadm5EC&pg=PA25&source=gbs_toc_r&cad=2#v=onepage&q&f=false}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=w:Eduardo Galeano|title=[[w:Open Veins of Latin America|Open Veins of Latin America: Five Centuries of the Pillage of a Continent]]|publisher=Monthly Review Press|page=38
|passage=The Indians of the Americas totaled no less than 70 million when the foreign conquerors appeared on the '''horizon'''; a century and a half later they had been reduced to 3.5 million.}}
# The range or limit of any dimension in which one exists.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|title=Thinking with Heidegger: Displacements|author=Miguel de Beistegui|page=157|ISBN=0253110637|passage=Only mortality, this irreducible and primordial '''horizon''', that very '''horizon''' which, in ''Being and Time'', Heidegger so compellingly revealed as the unsurpassable and defining possibility, remains.}}
# {{lb|en|geology}} A specific [[layer]] of [[soil]], or [[stratum]]
# {{lb|en|archaeology|mainly||US}} A cultural sub-period or level within a more encompassing time period.
# Any [[level]] [[line]] or [[surface]].
# {{lb|en|computer chess}} The point at which a [[computer chess]] [[algorithm]] stops searching for further moves.

