===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|dated}} A [[lodging]] for [[pilgrim]]s or the [[destitute]], normally provided by a [[monastic]] [[order]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|title=AA Book of British Villages|publisher=Drive Publications Ltd|page=261, about [[Llawhaden]]|text=At the west end of the street is the ruined 13th-century '''hospice''', known locally as Pilgrims' Rest. It was built by Bishop Beck for the use of travellers, and was used as place of rest by pilgrims on their way from [[Bardsey Island]] to St David's Cathedral.}} 
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The provision of [[palliative]] care for [[terminally]] [[ill]] patients, either at a specialized facility or at a residence, and support for the family, typically refraining from taking extraordinary measures to prolong life.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|publisher=Hospice & Palliative Nurses Association|title=Statement on the Scope and Standards of '''Hospice''' and Palliative Nursing Assistant Practice|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=K3-nDPqM6GQC&pg=PA2&dq=%22hospice%22%7C%22hospices%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=kd5QU6_hE4LukQWzhYGwBg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22hospice%22%7C%22hospices%22&f=false|page=2
|passage=The modern concept of '''hospice''' and palliative nursing has its roots in the hospice movement. Introduced to the United States in the early 1960s, '''hospice''' has grown from one organization in 1971 to more than 3500 organizations in 2001.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Iraida V. Carrion|title=End of Life Issues among Hispanics/Latinos|pageurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160920194834/https://books.google.com.au/books?id=W7gvFlE9VBIC&pg=PA52&dq=%22hospice%22%7C%22hospices%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=kd5QU6_hE4LukQWzhYGwBg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22hospice%22%7C%22hospices%22&f=false|page=52
|passage=Thus, the introduction of '''hospice''' services marked a new beginning, not only for the care of the dying, but also for the practice of medicine as a whole.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Kathleen Garces-Foley|chapter=Chapter 1: '''Hospice''' and the Politics of Spirituality|editors=Paul Bramadat; Harold Coward; Kelli I. Stajduhar|title=Spirituality in '''Hospice''' Palliative Care|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=8cMoAAAAQBAJ&pg=PA15&dq=%22hospice%22%7C%22hospices%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=kd5QU6_hE4LukQWzhYGwBg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22hospice%22%7C%22hospices%22&f=false|page=13
|passage=With the emergence of the modern '''hospice''' movement and its institutionalization in the 1970s, however, the religious dimension of '''hospice''' moved considerably away from its Christian roots. While it is widely agreed that '''hospice''' ought to be concerned with the spiritual needs of the dying, how to do this in a pluralistic society is far from clear.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Peter Baker|title=Jimmy Carter, 98, Opts for Hospice Care|work=The New York Times|date=2023-02-18|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/02/18/us/politics/jimmy-carter-hospice.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Former President Jimmy Carter, who at 98 is the longest living president in American history, has decided to forgo further medical treatment and will enter '''hospice''' care at his home in Georgia, the Carter Center announced on Saturday.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A specialized facility or organization offering palliative care for the terminally ill.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Robert J. Buchanan; James D. Minor|title=Legal Aspects of Health Care Reimbursement|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=dAH_7318k1MC&pg=PA97&dq=%22hospices%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=mupQU4qaOMiulQXn7IHgCQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22hospices%22&f=false|page=97
|passage=Medicare reimbursement of hospice care is likely to be affected by the apparent reluctance of '''hospices''' to participate in the Medicare program.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Andrea Fontana; Jennifer Reid Keene|title=Death and Dying in America|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=Bi36bL6wVj8C&pg=PA36&dq=%22hospices%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=mupQU4qaOMiulQXn7IHgCQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22hospices%22&f=false|page=36
|passage=A small number of '''hospices''' were opened in the US in the 1890s, but these institutions differed from the '''hospices''' that came into being in the second part of the twentieth century in the following ways.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=June L. Leishman|chapter=Chapter 6: Developments in end-of-life care|editor=June Leishman|title=Perspectives on Death and Dying|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=Evxs8Z_WsNIC&pg=PA87&dq=%22hospices%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=mupQU4qaOMiulQXn7IHgCQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22hospices%22&f=false|page=87
|passage=St Joseph's Hospice Association in Liverpool supports '''hospices''' in Guatemala, Honduras, Ecuador and Peru, as well as having links with '''hospices''' in India, Pakistan and Mexico that bear the same name.}}

